//Insertion sort  -> Insert element at before index 
// | Case                 | Time Complexity | Explanation                                              |
// | -------------------- | --------------- | -------------------------------------------------------- |
// | **Best Case**        | **O(n)**        | Array is already sorted; only one comparison per element |
// | **Average Case**     | **O(n²)**       | Elements usually need to be shifted                      |
// | **Worst Case**       | **O(n²)**       | Array is in reverse order; maximum shifting              |
// | **Space Complexity** | **O(1)**        | Sorts the array in-place                                 |

// When to use Insertion Sort?

// Insertion Sort is a good choice when:

// The array is small
// For small datasets, its simplicity can be useful.
// The data is nearly sorted 
// This is one of its biggest advantages. If only a few elements are out of place, it can be very efficient.
// Data arrives incrementally
// You can insert a new element into an already sorted portion without sorting everything again.
// You need an in-place algorithm
// It requires only O(1) extra space.
//Inplace sorting
arr = [4,3,2,1]

for(i=1;i<arr.length;i++){
    let key = arr[i]
    let j = i-1
    while(j>=0 && arr[j]> key){
        arr[j+1] = arr[j]
        j--
    }
    arr[j+1] = key
}

console.log(arr)
